{"text": "This is a narrated lecture on efficient LLM inference, the area exam survey by Chien Van Nguyen at the University of Oregon. Lesson one, from training to the inference stack, the expensive part of large language models used to be training. That is no longer the bill that matters. Training is paid once, then spread across an enormous number of queries. What you pay every day is inference, actually serving the model. Real-time products, agent loops, and multi-step reasoning have pushed the total cost of ownership onto inference. The survey's claim is that efficiency is not a bag of independent tricks, it is one co-designed stack, organized by where a method is inserted. Pillar one is architecture. You change what the model is, linear attention, state space models such as Mamba, hybrid stacks, mixture of experts, and native ways to shrink the key value cache, multi-query attention, grouped query attention, and multi-head Latent attention. Pillar two is decoding. The weights stay put. You change how tokens are produced, so one read of the weights can emit more than one token. Speculative decoding, diffusion language models, Jacobi inconsistency iteration, and dynamic early exit. Pillar three is compression. Fewer bits and fewer weights that actually fire. Quantization, cache quantization, sparsity, and distillation. You spend a quality budget. Pillar four is hardware and systems. This is what turns a specification into wall clock speed. FlashAttention, paged memory, continuous batching, and serving prefill on different machines from decode. Hold onto the split between two phases. Prefill reads the whole prompt at once, fills the cache, and is rich in arithmetic per byte. It is compute-bound. Decode then emits one token at a time. For every token, billions of parameters move from high-bandwidth memory into on-chip memory. Decode Is memory bandwidth bound? The survey leaves out training parallelism, parameter-efficient fine-tuning such as LoRA, and test-time compute, unless those change a physical bottleneck. How to study this exam: For every technique, name the bottleneck it attacks, say whether the output distribution is preserved exactly, and say what you pay. The payment is recall, quality, or engineering complexity. The key value cache is the object that all four pillars touch.", "language": "en", "duration": 147.888, "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.12, "end": 0.28}, {"text": "is", "start": 0.32, "end": 0.42}, {"text": "a", "start": 0.48, "end": 0.5}, {"text": "narrated", "start": 0.58, "end": 1.02}, {"text": "lecture", "start": 1.08, "end": 1.501}, {"text": "on", "start": 1.621, "end": 1.701}, {"text": "efficient", "start": 1.741, "end": 2.201}, {"text": "LLM", "start": 2.421, "end": 2.781}, {"text": "inference,", "start": 2.921, "end": 3.461}, {"text": "the", "start": 3.901, "end": 3.981}, {"text": "area", "start": 4.101, "end": 4.341}, {"text": "exam", "start": 4.441, "end": 4.822}, {"text": "survey", "start": 4.942, "end": 5.302}, {"text": "by", "start": 5.402, "end": 5.522}, {"text": "Chien", "start": 5.622, "end": 5.962}, {"text": "Van", "start": 6.042, "end": 6.222}, {"text": "Nguyen", "start": 6.262, "end": 6.642}, {"text": "at", "start": 6.862, "end": 6.942}, {"text": "the", "start": 6.962, "end": 7.042}, {"text": "University", "start": 7.122, "end": 7.623}, {"text": "of", "start": 7.683, "end": 7.743}, {"text": "Oregon.", "start": 7.863, "end": 8.203}, {"text": "Lesson", "start": 8.903, "end": 9.203}, {"text": "one,", "start": 9.363, "end": 9.543}, {"text": "from", "start": 10.063, "end": 10.223}, {"text": "training", "start": 10.283, "end": 10.704}, {"text": "to", "start": 10.764, "end": 10.844}, {"text": "the", "start": 10.884, "end": 10.964}, {"text": "inference", "start": 11.064, "end": 11.504}, {"text": "stack,", "start": 11.524, "end": 11.844}, {"text": "the", "start": 12.404, "end": 12.484}, {"text": "expensive", "start": 12.524, "end": 13.084}, {"text": "part", "start": 13.124, "end": 13.344}, 